AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) tend to generate buggy code, particularly by misusing APIs from unfamiliar libraries. Incorrect API usage can severely impact the functionality of the generated code. Current error detection and program repair methods fail to address API hallucinations, especially when facing data-scarcity such as private libraries. This paper introduces the StepwiseGraphFix framework, which detects API misuses in LLM-generated code by retrieving and comparing the code library's knowledge graph. Then it applies a stepwise refinement strategy to repair the API misuses via knowledge graph reasoning and iterative feedback. We constructed the UnifiedFixEval dataset for program repair in unseen libraries by integrating five existing code generation datasets, and validated our method against several other baselines on this dataset. Experimental results show that StepwiseGraphFix outperforms current state-of-the-art methods, achieving an $\mathbf{8. 1 \%}$ relative improvement in repair success rate and a 4.0 % relative improvement in test pass rate. Ablation studies further confirm each component's contribution to overall performance, and the multi-round repair mechanism improves repair quality.
