AB  - In recent years, Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable potential in Automated Program Repair (APR), with the mainstream paradigm being to prompt LLMs with buggy code and related information to generate repaired patches. However, the impact of different types of buggy code information on the repair performance of LLMs remains insufficiently explored and verified, which restricts the optimization of prompt construction and the improvement of APR efficiency. To address this research gap, we conduct a systematic empirical study on LLM-based APR, investigating six distinct types of buggy code information (BuggyCode, buggy code Description, suspected BuggyLine, Program Dependence Graph (PDG), TestFile, TestOutput) and constructing eight hierarchical information combinations (1 baseline + 5 singleinformation enhancements + 2 combined forms) for evaluation. We select six LLMs with diverse architectures, parameter scales and deployment modes, and conduct experiments on the QuixBugs Python subset to answer four core research questions. Experimental results reveal that code description and BuggyLine are universal high-value information for all models, while PDG and TestOutput only benefit high-capability models, and TestFile acts as low-value noise with inhibitory effects in most cases. High-capability commercial models exhibit significant synergistic effects with full-information combinations, achieving a maximum Repair Success Rate (RSR) of 97.5%, while low-capability models suffer from context overload due to redundant information. Current mainstream code-specialized LLMs outperform deep learning-based and classic LLM-based APR methods by a substantial margin on the benchmark dataset. Based on the findings, we provide actionable guidelines for information se-lection, model choice and prompt optimization for LLM-based APR, laying a solid empirical foundation for the further development of automated program repair technologies.
ER  - 


