AB  - Many automated debugging tools can repair faulty code but often fail to explain why their fixes make sense. This reduces developer trust and limits our ability to study how reasoning connects to repair success. We introduce , a lightweight framework and benchmark that links short natural-language explanations to verified fixes across well-known bug–fix datasets which has three main components: a Bug Analyzer that identifies likely fault locations and suggests possible causes, a Fix Generator that applies small, rule-based edits and records a concise rationale, and a Verifier that checks whether the fix works and measures how closely the explanation matches the result. We evaluate on QuixBugs, Defects4J, and a CodeXGLUE refinement split (Bugs2Fix). The system outputs detailed logs containing the buggy code, the reference fix, the generated fix, the reasoning text, and validation scores. In experiments, generated repairs closely matched reference fixes on Bugs2Fix (mean ≈ 0.97) and Defects4J placeholders (mean ≈ 0.87), while QuixBugs showed moderate variation (mean ≈ 0.80) due to its diverse algorithms. All runs finished quickly and produced reproducible metrics and plots. By combining reasoning with verification, provides a transparent, reproducible foundation for explainable debugging. It is simple to extend with more datasets, richer test cases, or large language model–based fix generators, paving the way for deeper research into reasoning-aware program repair.
ER  - 


