AB  - Software evolution in video games presents unique challenges due to rapid updates and player-driven development. However, there is a scarcity of empirical research that systematically analyzes these processes. This study addresses this gap by conducting a large-scale empirical analysis of software maintenance types in the live-service game sector. We applied Mining Software Repositories (MSR) techniques, coupled with a fine-tuned RoBERTa language model, to automatically classify over 31,000 changelog entries from 50 popular multiplayer competitive games on the Steam platform. Using the ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764:2022 standard as a framework, our classification reveals that game maintenance is dominated by Perfective updates (55.5%), which focus on enhancing gameplay and user experience. These are followed by Corrective updates (30.3%) for bug resolution and Additive updates (14.1%) for introducing new content. Adaptive updates are exceptionally rare (0.1%). Further analysis using time series and seasonal decomposition revealed structured and recurring update patterns, highlighting a cyclical and reactive maintenance model. Specifically, peaks in Corrective updates tend to follow the release of new features and enhancements, suggesting developers often adopt a "feature-centric, fix-later" approach. This research validates the use of automated changelog classification in this domain and provides an empirical foundation for developers to improve strategic update planning, anticipate bug-fixing workloads, and better manage the software lifecycle in live-service games.
